(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_dualnatureofradiationandmatter=[{id:"neet-2026-q014",pn:14,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric effect graphs",q:"A beam of light falls on a metal surface such that photo-electrons are generated. If power of the light source starts to decrease linearly with time t, then variation of the photocurrent I and magnitude of the stopping potential |V| with time is best represented by:",o:["I decreases linearly with t (straight line falling to zero); |V| decreases linearly with t","I is constant with t; |V| decreases linearly with t","I is constant with t; |V| is constant with t","I decreases linearly with t; |V| is constant with t"],c:3,s:`Power of the source decreases, so the number of photons per second decreases linearly with time. The source is the same, so the energy of each photon is the same.
Power P \u221D nE, so P \u221D n (number of photons per second).
So photocurrent I decreases linearly with time.
Photon energy is constant, so the stopping potential |V| stays constant with time.`},{id:"neet-2026-q023",pn:23,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric cells stopping potential",q:"A ray of light with wavelength \u03BB is incident on three different photoelectric cells namely 1, 2 and 3. The threshold wavelength of these photoelectric cells are \u03BB\u2081, \u03BB\u2082, and \u03BB\u2083, respectively and the magnitude of stopping potentials of these cells are V\u2081, V\u2082 and V\u2083, respectively. The relation between \u03BB and threshold wavelengths are \u03BB\u2081 < \u03BB, \u03BB\u2082 > \u03BB and \u03BB\u2083 >> \u03BB. The correct option is:",o:["V\u2081 = 0, V\u2082 > V\u2083","V\u2081 > V\u2082, V\u2083 = 0","V\u2081 < V\u2082, V\u2083 = 0","V\u2081 = 0, V\u2082 < V\u2083"],c:3,s:`An electron is ejected only if the photon energy is greater than the work function, \u03C6 = hc/\u03BB.
For cell 1, \u03BB\u2081 < \u03BB, so \u03C6\u2081 > \u03C6 (work function of cell 1 is greater than the photon energy).
So no electron is ejected and V\u2081 = 0.
Einstein equation: photon energy \u03C6 = \u03C6\u2080 + eV.
For cells 2 and 3: \u03BB\u2082 > \u03BB and \u03BB\u2083 >> \u03BB, so \u03C6 > \u03C6\u2082 and \u03C6 >> \u03C6\u2083 (work functions are smaller than photon energy).
So V\u2083 > V\u2082.`},{id:"neet-2026-q040",pn:40,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun \xB7 Re-exam",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Momentum of photon and electron",q:`A photon and an electron, each of 20 eV energy, move in free space. The ratio of linear momentum of electron p\u2091 to that of photon pPh, p\u2091/pPh is:
[Take speed of light = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 ms\u207B\xB9, charge of electron = \u20131.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C and mass of electron = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg]`,o:["1/250","225","275","2/450"],c:1,s:`E\u2091 = EPh = 20 eV.
For the electron p\u2091 = \u221A(2m\u2091E\u2091), for the photon pPh = EPh/c.
p\u2091/pPh = (\u221A(2m\u2091E\u2091)/EPh)\xB7c = \u221A(2m\u2091/EPh)\xB7c.
p\u2091/pPh = \u221A((2 \xD7 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9)/(20 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079)) \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u2078.
= (3/4) \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u2078.
= 900/4 = 225.`}]})();
